we're in the  galaxy de bataille and if anyone thought  that  war in europe belonged to history  for the last two weeks we've seen that  the choice by  russia under president putin was to  bring war back to europe in the face of  this and the unheard of violence  of russia against ukraine and its  population is a tragic turning point for  our  history we're all aware of the fact that  it's a turning point as well for our  societies for our peoples and for our  european project  for the last two weeks our response  ladies and gentlemen this has just been  said we've responded through unity  rapidity and a spirit of responsibility  choices were made very quickly  all 27 with our institutions and  coordinated with all of our partners  in the context of nato and g7  diplomatic workers made it possible to  isolate russia israelia recently at the  general assembly of the united nations  for two weeks now ukrainian men and  women have been fighting courageously to  defend their freedom their democracy  their sovereignty and the integrity of  their land  europeans have a clear position  unanimous  and firm condemnation of the russian  aggression  at all international levels and we've  grown full consequences of this  in trailer with the resolution that was  adopted the general assembly  support to ukraine together we've agreed  to deliver  hardware not just humanitarian material  but also weapons and we've propose today  that the european  facility  be attitude to the tune of some 500  million which is going to allow us to go  further in this  line  countries that in the past were  traditionally neutral sweden for  instance have decided for the very first  time  in the for the first time in decades  they will take an active role in  the support to ukraine we also have  economic support humanitarian support  this will be contingent continued some 2  million refugees have been welcomed  madame vanderlian has referred to this  and i'd like to thank the populations of  the neighboring countries poland hungary  romania  and moldova as well  and obviously france will be playing its  role and i would hope that we will be  able to have the necessary mechanisms of  solidarity that can be used in this  overall approach as of the very outset  we also deployed massive unheard of  sanctions and this was done very quickly  second  fourth six days of conflict they were  marked by european decisions  of these  unprecedented sanctions  we're willing to adopt other sanctions  and all options are on the table  the point of our discussion is to order  is to prepare ourselves for these  sanctions if they were  able to call a halt to the attack and  the aggression and could prepare us also  for  the consequences in the coming weeks and  months  our initial objective is of course a  rapid return to peace  starting with the ceasefire  followed by a rapid withdrawal of  russian troops and that is why keeping  up pressure on russia  and we're also maintaining a very  demanding dialogue  in particular in conjunction with the  german chancellor so the support to  solidarity for ukraine  is what kept us very busy particularly  yesterday  we support ukraine today and we will do  that for as long as the war lasts and we  will be there  for the reconstruction of this  destruction brought about by russia we  will be there to help it to stand up and  stay standing  the message that we've sent out to the  ukrainian population  is that the path to our europe is open  to them their fight for freedom and  democracy for the values that we all  share has demonstrated that ukraine is  indeed a member of our european family  we also want the  applications  made by moldova and georgia as well  be examined in line with our treaty by  the european commission  so that  the acceptability  can be determined  in line with the provisions of our  treaty  the versailles declaration  the agenda that we've been discussing  today  linked to the fact that sovereignty in  europe  which  might have been thought of by some as a  slogan  or a sort of french fantasy  is seen by all today as being  crucial  the sovereignty this is  the strategy of independence has been  there at the very start also the  pandemic we've seen what will have to be  done it's there when  we have to produce  and deliver vaccines for ourselves and  for other countries we've seen it when  we came out of the crisis uh the start  of the recovery when we saw what was  happening as we got to the semi  conductors when we had delegated  production to the other parts of the  world how difficult it was to return to  the manufacturing of cars for instance  and we're seeing  it in the context of this crisis where  we can see  how our food our energy our defense  are all issues of sovereignty we can  cooperate  we want to be open to the world but we  want to choose our partners and not  depend on any that's what we mean by  sovereignty it's not protectionism it's  not a question of closing the door it's  a question of having the possibility of  staying open without being dependent so  the vasa agenda has set out the next few  months work along those lines as regards  defense  we want to be able to define the  investments that we need and several of  us are already at the level of two  percent of gdp invested in defense  others have further efforts to make but  we're going to make coordinated efforts  we're going to identify our needs and  budgetary terms we're going to identify  capabilities that are required we'll  also identify our needs when it comes to  possible new conflicts whether we're  talking about shipping maritime space  and also identify  what we need in order to develop the  european industrial  tissue  if we're going to import more in the way  of hardware from elsewhere then there's  not not much point pursuing this sort of  sovereignty we need to think in terms of  partnerships yes but we have to be able  to decide  to consolidate and to construct  a defense industry with fewer standards  but corresponding to an integrated  market  so the number of decisions that we've  already taken  ago germany in turn decided to make  historic investments  in the country for the country  and denmark made a historic choice  deciding  to  ask the people if they want to come back  to the european security and defense  project you can see that everywhere  around the european continent historic  choices are being made and this is a  major turning point  we need to have the organization so that  we can build this joint  ambition in defense and this is what we  have asked the commission to do so that  by may  uh when we would know that have an  extraordinary meeting of the council  before the  summit in june in the summit meeting in  june we will work up a plan which is  full for uh investment to get our  industries up to the right  capacity and capability level  and then  we've got the question of  of climate strategy we've started  building our own independence getting  out of  fossil energy as a matter of becoming  independent and we know that moving away  from coal was a special punk then we  moved to gas but that's gas that we have  imported and are still importing from  russia  the situation that we're in at the  moment and which we will have tomorrow  means we have to reassess this strategy  and be even more ambitious with our  climate plans  and for that  we will have a  a check on this by late march and see  how we can provide the support for our  populations for president and we're  going to work on this at the commission  with concrete  objectives for prices and for uh and for  refining so that we can guarantee their  mechanisms  which means that we're not  subjected to the contingencies of the  market or speculation of the market in  particular in crisis situations many of  you have already  taken steps to protect  our citizens and companies and we need  to have european choices and in the two  weeks to come we will  do our best so that we can stand up to  the price shock which comes from the  geopolitical  context prices were going up before the  war and they're continuing to increase  because of the war  course we have the sanctions which are  coming in and we must prepare ourselves  so that we are not so sensitive to the  dependency on russia and this is what  we've  scheduled for may for the commission as  the two presidents have pointed out we  have the plan to step out of the  dependency on fossil fuels from russia  so that we can get up by 2027 so that we  are no longer dependent on uh russian uh  gas oil and coal that's by 2027 this  means there has to be new investment new  facilities  so that we can um  support our choices for renewables and  for nuclear energy you can see that  march and may will be very busy  for energy subjects  and there are other issues that we will  have to work together on  to ensure that we are independent and  sovereign there's the food strategy  whether we're talking about wheat or  maize or grain crops in general we know  that ukraine and russia are two huge  markets  and europe has already been upset by the  war on the on the fruit supply market  and this will be even more so  in 12 to 18 months  with the impact on ukraine and crops  there and there will be a major  disturbance to food supplies in  in field feed supplies so we have to  strike a new balance and look at food  production so that we can have  autonomy for our food production and for  protein production and we must reassess  the strategy for africa because the  number of african countries are going to  be hit by famines in the next 12 to 18  months  because of this  [Music]  sovereignty war other commodities  raw materials for rare materials  and i've mentioned the question of  semiconductors the health care i won't  go through the whole list but we have  this agenda we will be going through  this in the months to come with very  concrete proposals for investments which  will make it possible for to have more  sovereign and more independent europe  we will be  encouraging  and we're going to look at the single  market in different sectors  clear and the european uh  the central european bank will be  following its work and will be doing  this very clearly in the current  geopolitical geopolitical context and  will have national investment policies  to protect us from the impact of the war  to fund our solidarity to fund our  resistance to the impact of the war and  to defend our sovereignty but the next  point is that we  need  means of  funding these major strategies funding  from europe and  these are the things i mentioned and  this is why we need to have joint  funding we need to coordinate our  investments and we should do it in a  european context so that we don't see  the financial markets being broken up  and  and different forms of sovereign debt  and this is what we've been  working on as our strategy and we'll be  working on it for the months to come and  here we will have to be  acting together acting swiftly and with  the spirit of responsibility  the ukrainians have given us  an example which is quite unique the  example of courage  we are not waging war  europe is not waging war on russia but  we're supporting  ukraine and we're sanctioning russia but  we must have the courage to make  historic decisions  to see that the defense of democracy and  our values has a cost the choice of  independence has a cost  and sometimes we have to go back on some  of the dogmas that we've been following  for years the different habits and the  ways of organizing things which we had  and i can tell you that the discussions  we had yesterday and today  have  realized this awareness and we can see  there's a real ambition here from the  europeans here in the site to advance in  that direction and i would like to  conclude on that  we started the work yesterday by  extending thanks to some of the  colleagues i would like to say this to  the president of the commission because  here in versailles it's a very historic  site which the french of course love  above all and which we're very proud of  and we see it it's known around the  world but it's a site which is also the  site of diplomatic defeats  it's the site where we lost peace  we were there  with an ambition to  humiliate and divide europe  with the treaty of vexa  and despite  the difficulties we've had here today  with the emotions and  different attitudes we did have a  unanimous  agreement on the fact that the only way  of protecting our continent was through  unity through courage and unity that's  the most important thing  thank you

